# The other side of infer_order_java: a `use java` class name is subtracted
# from the call graph only while it really names the Java class. A constructor
# of this module claims the spelling first (check_method_call consults
# `ctors_by_name` before the static arm), so `Integer.tag()` below is an
# ordinary UFCS call to the local `tag` and its edge has to survive. `describe`
# is declared before `tag` on purpose: a dropped edge checks it first, against
# the placeholder signature every unchecked inferred function starts with
# (return type `TyError`, effect `!io`), and the annotated pure `shown` is what
# reports it. The golden is empty on purpose -- nothing here is recursive.
#
# This lives apart from infer_order_java because the two cannot share a module:
# once `Integer` is a constructor here, it is no longer the Java class there.

use java "java.lang.Integer"

type Boxed = Integer

fn describe() = Integer.tag() + 0

fn tag(b: Boxed) = 7

pub fn shown() -> Int = describe()

pub fn main() -> Unit !io = println(to_string(shown()))
